### Repository Root Structure

The EM Ecosystem monorepo contains backend, dashboard, and website as independent projects within a single git repository:

```
em-ecosystem-code/                       # Git repository root
├── .gitignore                           # Shared gitignore (node_modules, dist, .next, .env, etc.)
├── README.md                            # Repository overview and setup instructions
│
├── nexacore-api/                        # Backend — NestJS 11 API
│   ├── package.json                     # @em-ecosystem/nexacore-api
│   ├── src/                             # NestJS source code (modules, controllers, services)
│   ├── prisma/                          # Prisma schema and migrations
│   └── test/                            # E2E tests
│
├── nexacore-dashboard/                  # Dashboard — Next.js 14 (App Router)
│   ├── package.json                     # @em-ecosystem/nexacore-dashboard
│   ├── src/                             # Next.js source code (app, components, lib)
│   └── public/                          # Static assets
│
└── nexacore-website/                    # Public website (planned — not yet implemented)
    ├── package.json                     # @em-ecosystem/nexacore-website
    └── src/
```

**Key conventions:**
- Each project (`nexacore-api/`, `nexacore-dashboard/`, `nexacore-website/`) has its own `package.json`, `node_modules/`, and `tsconfig.json`
- Dependencies are managed independently — run `npm install` inside each project directory
- All commands (`npm test`, `npm run build`, `npx prisma generate`) must be executed from inside the respective project directory
- The `.gitignore` at repository root applies to all projects
- No root-level `package.json` — this is not an npm workspace; each project is independent

**Package naming convention:**
- npm scope: `@em-ecosystem/`
- Backend API: `@em-ecosystem/nexacore-api`
- Dashboard frontend: `@em-ecosystem/nexacore-dashboard`
- Public website: `@em-ecosystem/nexacore-website`
- Future shared UI library: `@em-ecosystem/ui`
- Satellite apps follow: `@em-ecosystem/{satellite-name}-api` / `@em-ecosystem/{satellite-name}-dashboard`

## Domain Modeling Principles

### Entities and Interfaces

Entities represent core domain objects with a persistent identity. In NestJS with Prisma, entities are defined as TypeScript interfaces that mirror the Prisma schema, with helper types for safe API exposure.

**Pattern:**
```typescript
// src/users/entities/user.entity.ts
export interface User {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  passwordHash: string | null;
  role: Role;
  provider: Provider;
  providerId: string | null;
  emailVerified: boolean;
  failedAttempts: number;
  lockedUntil: Date | null;
  refreshToken: string | null;
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
}

// SafeUser excludes sensitive fields from API responses
export type SafeUser = Omit<User, 'passwordHash' | 'refreshToken'>;

export function toSafeUser(user: User): SafeUser {
  const { passwordHash, refreshToken, ...safeUser } = user;
  return safeUser;
}
```

**Multi-entity example:**
```typescript
// src/projects/entities/project.entity.ts
export interface Project {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  slug: string;
  description: string | null;
  status: ProjectStatus;
  ownerId: string;
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
}

// ProjectWithMembers — includes related data for detail views
export type ProjectWithMembers = Project & {
  members: (ProjectMember & { user: SafeUserSummary })[];
};
```

**Best Practice**: Entities should define their shape via interfaces. Use Omit/Pick types to create safe projections. Use intersection types (`&`) for common query result patterns that include related data. Never expose sensitive fields (passwords, tokens) in API responses. The complete entity catalog is documented in `data-model.md`.

### Value Objects and Enums

Value Objects represent domain concepts without identity. In NestJS, these are typically modeled as TypeScript enums or union types.

```typescript
// src/users/enums/role.enum.ts
export enum Role {
  USER = 'USER',
  ADMIN = 'ADMIN',
  SUPERADMIN = 'SUPERADMIN',
}

// src/users/enums/provider.enum.ts
export enum Provider {
  LOCAL = 'LOCAL',
  GOOGLE = 'GOOGLE',
  GITHUB = 'GITHUB',
}
```

```typescript
// src/projects/enums/project-status.enum.ts
export enum ProjectStatus {
  ACTIVE = 'ACTIVE',
  ARCHIVED = 'ARCHIVED',
  SUSPENDED = 'SUSPENDED',
}

// src/projects/enums/member-role.enum.ts
export enum MemberRole {
  OWNER = 'OWNER',
  ADMIN = 'ADMIN',
  MEMBER = 'MEMBER',
  VIEWER = 'VIEWER',
}
```

**Best Practice**: Use enums for finite sets of domain values. Enums are validated at both TypeScript compile time and Prisma schema level. The complete enum catalog (12 enums) is documented in `data-model.md`.

### Services and Business Logic

Services contain business logic and are the primary actors in the application layer. They are injected via NestJS's dependency injection system.

**Pattern:**
```typescript
@Injectable()
export class AuthService {
  constructor(
    private readonly usersService: UsersService,
    private readonly jwtService: JwtService,
  ) {}

  async register(dto: RegisterDto): Promise<{ accessToken: string; refreshToken: string; user: SafeUser }> {
    const existingUser = await this.usersService.findByEmail(dto.email);
    if (existingUser) {
      throw new ConflictException('Email already registered');
    }

    const passwordHash = await bcrypt.hash(dto.password, BCRYPT_ROUNDS);
    const user = await this.usersService.create({ email: dto.email, passwordHash });
    const tokens = await this.generateTokens(user);

    return { ...tokens, user: toSafeUser(user) };
  }
}
```

**Multi-entity service example with transactions:**
```typescript
@Injectable()
export class ProjectsService {
  constructor(
    private readonly prisma: PrismaService,
    private readonly notificationsService: NotificationsService,
  ) {}

  async create(dto: CreateProjectDto, userId: string): Promise<Project> {
    const slug = this.generateSlug(dto.name);

    return this.prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => {
      const project = await tx.project.create({
        data: { name: dto.name, slug, description: dto.description, ownerId: userId },
      });

      // Automatically add creator as OWNER member
      await tx.projectMember.create({
        data: { projectId: project.id, userId, role: MemberRole.OWNER },
      });

      // Create default FREE subscription
      await tx.subscription.create({
        data: { projectId: project.id, plan: BillingPlan.FREE },
      });

      return project as Project;
    });
  }
}
```

**Best Practice**: Services should depend on other services via constructor injection, never on concrete implementations directly. Business rules (duplicate checking, password hashing, token generation) live in services, not controllers. Use Prisma `$transaction()` for multi-entity atomic operations.

### Data Access with PrismaService

Instead of the repository pattern with interfaces, EM NexaCore uses PrismaService as a global injectable that extends PrismaClient. Data access methods live in dedicated services (e.g., UsersService) that inject PrismaService.

**PrismaService Pattern:**
```typescript
// src/prisma/prisma.service.ts
@Injectable()
export class PrismaService extends PrismaClient implements OnModuleInit, OnModuleDestroy {
  async onModuleInit(): Promise<void> {
    await this.$connect();
  }

  async onModuleDestroy(): Promise<void> {
    await this.$disconnect();
  }
}

// src/prisma/prisma.module.ts
@Global()
@Module({
  providers: [PrismaService],
  exports: [PrismaService],
})
export class PrismaModule {}
```

**Data Access Service Pattern:**
```typescript
@Injectable()
export class UsersService {
  constructor(private readonly prisma: PrismaService) {}

  async findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null> {
    return this.prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email } }) as Promise<User | null>;
  }

  async create(data: { email: string; passwordHash: string }): Promise<User> {
    try {
      return await this.prisma.user.create({ data: { ...data, provider: Provider.LOCAL } }) as User;
    } catch (error: unknown) {
      if (error instanceof Error && 'code' in error && (error as { code: string }).code === 'P2002') {
        throw new ConflictException('Email already registered');
      }
      throw new InternalServerErrorException('Failed to create user');
    }
  }
}
```

**Multi-Entity Operations**: For operations that span multiple tables (e.g., creating a Project with its owner membership and default subscription), use Prisma's `$transaction()` to ensure atomicity. See the ProjectsService.create example above.

**Best Practice**: PrismaService is `@Global()` so it's available everywhere without re-importing. Handle Prisma error codes (P2002 = unique constraint, P2025 = not found) and translate them to appropriate HTTP exceptions.

### Additional Recommendations

**Use of Factories**

Factories are useful to encapsulate the logic of creating complex objects, ensuring that all created objects comply with domain rules from the moment of creation.

**Recommendation**: Implement factory functions or static creation methods for entities that require specific initialization logic or validation.

**Domain Events Integration**

Domain events are an important part of domain modeling and can be used to handle side effects of domain operations in a decoupled manner.

**Recommendation**: Consider implementing a domain event system using NestJS's built-in event emitter (`@nestjs/event-emitter`) that allows services to publish events that other system components can handle without tight coupling.

## SOLID and DRY Principles

### SOLID Principles

SOLID principles are five object-oriented design principles that help create more understandable, flexible, and maintainable systems.

#### Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

Each class should have a single responsibility or reason to change.

**Before:**
```typescript
// A controller that handles validation, business logic, AND response formatting
@Controller('auth')
export class AuthController {
  async register(@Body() body: any) {
    if (!body.email.includes('@')) throw new Error('Invalid email');
    const hash = await bcrypt.hash(body.password, 12);
    const user = await this.prisma.user.create({ data: { email: body.email, passwordHash: hash } });
    return user;
  }
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
// Controller only handles HTTP concerns
@Controller('auth')
export class AuthController {
  constructor(private readonly authService: AuthService) {}

  @Post('register')
  @HttpCode(HttpStatus.CREATED)
  async register(@Body() registerDto: RegisterDto) {
    return this.authService.register(registerDto);
  }
}

// Service handles business logic
@Injectable()
export class AuthService {
  async register(dto: RegisterDto) {
    // validation is handled by ValidationPipe + DTO
    // business logic lives here
  }
}

// DTO handles validation
export class RegisterDto {
  @IsEmail({}, { message: 'Invalid email format' })
  email: string;
}
```

**Explanation**: The controller only handles HTTP concerns, the service handles business logic, and the DTO handles validation — each class has a single responsibility.

**Best Practice**: In NestJS, SRP is enforced naturally by the module → controller → service → DTO pattern. Validation belongs in DTOs, business logic in services, HTTP handling in controllers.

#### Open/Closed Principle (OCP)

Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification.

**Before:**
```typescript
// Adding new auth methods requires modifying the service
@Injectable()
export class AuthService {
  async login(provider: string, credentials: any) {
    if (provider === 'local') { /* local login */ }
    else if (provider === 'google') { /* google login */ }
    else if (provider === 'github') { /* github login */ }
    // Adding a new provider means modifying this method
  }
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
// Each strategy handles its own authentication — extend by adding new strategies
@Injectable()
export class JwtStrategy extends PassportStrategy(Strategy) { /* JWT validation */ }

@Injectable()
export class GoogleStrategy extends PassportStrategy(Strategy, 'google') { /* Google OAuth */ }

@Injectable()
export class GitHubStrategy extends PassportStrategy(Strategy, 'github') { /* GitHub OAuth */ }

// To add a new provider, create a new strategy class — no existing code modified
```

**Explanation**: The Passport strategy pattern allows adding new authentication methods (e.g., Microsoft, Apple) by creating new strategy classes without modifying existing ones.

**Best Practice**: Use NestJS's module system and Passport strategies to extend functionality without modifying existing code. Guards and interceptors also follow OCP naturally.

#### Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

Objects of a derived class should be replaceable with objects of the base class without altering the program's functionality.

**Before:**
```typescript
// Guard that cannot completely fulfill the base contract
class TemporaryAuthGuard extends AuthGuard('jwt') {
  canActivate() {
    throw new Error("Not implemented for temporary users");
  }
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
// Guard that provides an appropriate implementation respecting the contract
@Injectable()
export class JwtAuthGuard extends AuthGuard('jwt') {
  // Inherits canActivate() and provides proper JWT validation
}

@Injectable()
export class RolesGuard implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    // Provides a proper implementation of the CanActivate contract
    const requiredRoles = this.reflector.getAllAndOverride<Role[]>(ROLES_KEY, [
      context.getHandler(),
      context.getClass(),
    ]);
    if (!requiredRoles) return true;
    const user = context.switchToHttp().getRequest().user;
    return requiredRoles.includes(user.role);
  }
}
```

**Explanation**: Both guards implement the `CanActivate` interface correctly, allowing them to be used interchangeably with `@UseGuards()`.

**Best Practice**: Continue using composition and interface implementation. NestJS guards, interceptors, and pipes all follow LSP through well-defined interfaces (CanActivate, NestInterceptor, PipeTransform).

#### Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

Many specific interfaces are better than a single general interface.

**Before:**
```typescript
// A large interface that small clients don't fully use
interface UserOperations {
  create(): Promise<User>;
  findByEmail(): Promise<User>;
  updateRefreshToken(): Promise<void>;
  incrementFailedAttempts(): Promise<User>;
  lockAccount(): Promise<void>;
  resetFailedAttempts(): Promise<void>;
  findOrCreateByOAuth(): Promise<User>;
  sendEmail(): Promise<void>;
  generateReport(): Promise<void>;
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
// Segregated interfaces for different concerns
interface UserFinder {
  findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null>;
  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;
}

interface UserCreator {
  create(data: { email: string; passwordHash: string }): Promise<User>;
}

interface AccountSecurity {
  incrementFailedAttempts(userId: string): Promise<User>;
  lockAccount(userId: string): Promise<void>;
  resetFailedAttempts(userId: string): Promise<void>;
}

// UsersService implements only what it needs
@Injectable()
export class UsersService implements UserFinder, UserCreator, AccountSecurity {
  // implementation
}
```

**Explanation**: Interfaces are segregated into smaller operations, allowing classes to implement only the interfaces they need.

**Best Practice**: Define granular interfaces for service classes. In NestJS, this is especially useful for testing — you can mock only the interface subset a test requires.

#### Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.

**Before:**
```typescript
// Direct dependency on a concrete implementation
@Injectable()
export class AuthService {
  private prisma = new PrismaClient();

  async findUser(email: string) {
    return this.prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email } });
  }
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
// Depends on injected abstractions
@Injectable()
export class AuthService {
  constructor(
    private readonly usersService: UsersService,  // injected, not instantiated
    private readonly jwtService: JwtService,       // injected, not instantiated
  ) {}

  async findUser(email: string) {
    return this.usersService.findByEmail(email);
  }
}
```

**Explanation**: `AuthService` depends on injected services, not concrete implementations. NestJS's DI container manages the lifecycle and wiring.

**Best Practice**: Always use constructor injection in NestJS. Never instantiate dependencies directly (`new Service()`). This enables easy mocking in tests and flexible module composition.

### DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)

The DRY principle focuses on reducing duplication in code. Each piece of knowledge should have a single, unambiguous, and authoritative representation within a system.

**Before:**
```typescript
// Repeated validation logic in multiple endpoints
@Post('register')
async register(@Body() body: any) {
  if (!body.email || !body.email.includes('@')) throw new BadRequestException('Invalid email');
  if (!body.password || body.password.length < 8) throw new BadRequestException('Password too short');
  // register logic
}

@Post('login')
async login(@Body() body: any) {
  if (!body.email || !body.email.includes('@')) throw new BadRequestException('Invalid email');
  if (!body.password) throw new BadRequestException('Password required');
  // login logic
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
// Validation centralized in DTOs via class-validator decorators
export class RegisterDto {
  @IsEmail({}, { message: 'Invalid email format' })
  email: string;

  @IsString()
  @MinLength(8, { message: 'Password must be at least 8 characters' })
  password: string;
}

// Controller uses DTO — validation happens automatically via global ValidationPipe
@Post('register')
async register(@Body() registerDto: RegisterDto) {
  return this.authService.register(registerDto);
}
```

**Explanation**: Validation is centralized in DTO classes with decorators. The global `ValidationPipe` applies validation automatically, eliminating duplicated validation logic across endpoints.

**Best Practice**: Use NestJS's declarative patterns (DTOs, guards, decorators) to centralize cross-cutting concerns. Shared logic should live in common modules (e.g., `common/decorators/`, `common/filters/`).

### Code Reuse & Duplication

> Added as part of SCRUM-357 (audit-2026-05-06 DU-04 recurrence prevention). Reference: CISQ ASCMM-MNT-19, ISO 25010 Reusability, `/audit` Phase 10c.

#### Rule of Three (mandatory)

When you find yourself copying a block of **≥10 lines** from another file for the **third time**, STOP and extract a helper. The first copy is fine. The second copy is a heuristic warning. The third copy is a refactor requirement.

#### Pre-extraction checklist (before duplicating ≥10 lines)

1. Search the existing codebase: does a helper already do this?
   - `src/common/utils/` for cross-cutting helpers
   - Module-internal `services/` for domain logic
2. If a helper exists, use it and update its signature if needed.
3. If no helper exists and this is the SECOND occurrence, leave a `// TODO(refactor): potential extraction with <other-file>` comment.
4. If this is the THIRD occurrence, extract before completing the ticket.

#### Post-refactor rule (when splitting a service into smaller ones)

After splitting a class/service into multiple files (e.g., facade pattern, SRP refactor), run:

```bash
npx jscpd <module>/ --min-lines 5 --min-tokens 50
```

to catch duplication introduced by the split. Resolve before commit.

#### Automated enforcement

A `jscpd` check runs in the repo-root pre-commit hook on staged `.ts` files. New cross-file clones above the threshold (≥10 lines / ≥50 tokens for production code) fail the commit with an actionable message. Override is possible with `--no-verify` but requires a justification in the commit body.

## Coding Standards

### Language and Naming Conventions

**All code, comments, and documentation must be written in English.** This is a mandatory requirement for all technical artifacts in the project.

- **Variable Naming**: Use camelCase for variables and functions (e.g., `userId`, `findUserById`)
- **Class Naming**: Use PascalCase for classes, interfaces, and DTOs (e.g., `User`, `AuthService`, `RegisterDto`)
- **Constants Naming**: Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants (e.g., `BCRYPT_ROUNDS`, `MAX_FAILED_ATTEMPTS`)
- **Enum Naming**: Use PascalCase for enum names and UPPER_CASE for values (e.g., `Role.ADMIN`)
- **File Naming**: Use kebab-case for NestJS files (e.g., `auth.service.ts`, `jwt-auth.guard.ts`, `register.dto.ts`)

**Examples:**

```typescript
// Good: All in English, NestJS naming conventions
@Injectable()
export class UsersService {
  constructor(private readonly prisma: PrismaService) {}

  async findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null> {
    return this.prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email } }) as Promise<User | null>;
  }
}

// Avoid: Non-English comments or names
@Injectable()
export class ServicioUsuarios {
  async buscarPorEmail(correo: string): Promise<Usuario | null> {
    // Buscar usuario por email en la base de datos
  }
}
```

**Error Messages and Logs:**

All user-facing error messages MUST use the centralized `ErrorMessages` constants from `src/common/constants/error-messages.ts`. Never use inline string literals for error messages thrown to clients.

```typescript
import { ErrorMessages } from '../common/constants/error-messages';

// Good: Use ErrorMessages constants (English, generic, no information disclosure)
throw new UnauthorizedException(ErrorMessages.auth.INVALID_CREDENTIALS);
throw new BadRequestException(ErrorMessages.mfa.OPERATION_NOT_AVAILABLE);

// Avoid: Inline strings that leak internal state
throw new ConflictException('Email already registered');     // reveals email existence
throw new ForbiddenException('Account is deactivated');      // reveals account state
throw new ForbiddenException('Insufficient permissions. Required: users:write'); // reveals permission names
```

**Error message rules (OWASP ASVS, CWE-200/203/209):**
- Public endpoints: maximum genericness — never reveal user existence, account state, or feature enrollment
- Authenticated endpoints: may reference the resource type but not internal state
- Never expose: email existence, MFA enrollment status, permission keys, lockout escalation levels, SUPERADMIN role name, token reuse detection, CSRF validation stages
- Use `Retry-After` HTTP header (not response body) for rate limit/lockout timing
- Audit logs should still capture specific reasons internally (fire-and-forget)

### TypeScript Usage

- **Strict Mode**: Always enable strict mode in `tsconfig.json`
- **Type Definitions**: Use explicit types for function parameters and return values
- **Interfaces**: Define interfaces for complex data structures
- **Avoid `any`**: Use `unknown` or specific types instead of `any` when possible
- **Decorator Metadata**: Enable `emitDecoratorMetadata` and `experimentalDecorators` for NestJS

```typescript
// Good: Explicit types with NestJS patterns
async findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> {
  return this.prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id } }) as Promise<User | null>;
}

// Avoid: Using any
async processData(data: any): Promise<any> {
  // implementation
}
```

### Error Handling

- **Use NestJS HttpException subclasses**: Use built-in exceptions for standard HTTP errors
- **Global Exception Filter**: Use a global filter for consistent error response formatting
- **Descriptive Messages**: Provide descriptive error messages for debugging

**Available HttpException subclasses:**
```typescript
throw new BadRequestException('Validation failed');          // 400
throw new UnauthorizedException('Invalid credentials');      // 401
throw new ForbiddenException('Access denied');               // 403
throw new NotFoundException('User not found');               // 404
throw new ConflictException('Email already registered');     // 409
throw new InternalServerErrorException('Failed to create user'); // 500
```

**In services:**
```typescript
async login(dto: LoginDto) {
  const user = await this.usersService.findByEmail(dto.email);
  if (!user) {
    throw new UnauthorizedException('Invalid credentials');
  }

  if (user.lockedUntil && user.lockedUntil > new Date()) {
    throw new ForbiddenException('Account locked. Try again later.');
  }

  // Business logic continues...
}
```

**Best Practice**: All user-facing errors must use `ErrorMessages` constants. See `src/common/constants/error-messages.ts` for the complete registry. Never use inline strings that could leak internal state.

### Validation Patterns

- **Use DTOs with class-validator**: Define validation rules declaratively on DTO properties
- **Global ValidationPipe**: Apply validation globally in `main.ts`
- **Custom Messages**: Provide user-friendly validation error messages

**DTO Validation:**
```typescript
import { IsEmail, IsString, MinLength, Matches } from 'class-validator';

export class RegisterDto {
  @IsEmail({}, { message: 'Invalid email format' })
  email: string;

  @IsString()
  @MinLength(8, { message: 'Password must be at least 8 characters' })
  @Matches(/(?=.*[a-z])/, { message: 'Password must contain at least one lowercase letter' })
  @Matches(/(?=.*[A-Z])/, { message: 'Password must contain at least one uppercase letter' })
  @Matches(/(?=.*\d)/, { message: 'Password must contain at least one number' })
  @Matches(/(?=.*[@$!%*?&])/, { message: 'Password must contain at least one special character' })
  password: string;
}
```

**Global ValidationPipe Configuration:**
```typescript
// main.ts
app.useGlobalPipes(
  new ValidationPipe({
    whitelist: true,              // Strip unknown properties
    forbidNonWhitelisted: true,   // Reject unknown properties
    transform: true,              // Auto-transform types
  }),
);
```

### Logging Standards

- **Use NestJS Logger**: Use the built-in `Logger` class from `@nestjs/common`
- **Log Levels**: Use appropriate log levels (log, error, warn, debug, verbose)
- **Structured Logging**: Include relevant context in log messages

```typescript
import { Logger } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class AuthService {
  private readonly logger = new Logger(AuthService.name);

  async register(dto: RegisterDto) {
    this.logger.log(`User registration attempt: ${dto.email}`);
    // ... business logic
    this.logger.log(`User registered successfully: ${user.id}`);
  }
}
```

## API Design Standards

### REST Endpoints

- **RESTful Naming**: Use RESTful conventions for endpoint naming
- **HTTP Methods**: Use appropriate HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH)
- **Resource-Based URLs**: URLs should represent resources, not actions
- **NestJS Decorators**: Use `@Controller()`, `@Get()`, `@Post()`, `@Put()`, `@Delete()` decorators

```typescript
@Controller('auth')
export class AuthController {
  @Post('register')      // POST /auth/register
  @HttpCode(HttpStatus.CREATED)
  async register(@Body() dto: RegisterDto) { }

  @Post('login')          // POST /auth/login
  @HttpCode(HttpStatus.OK)
  async login(@Body() dto: LoginDto) { }

  @Get('me')              // GET /auth/me
  @UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)
  getMe(@Request() req: { user: SafeUser }) { }

  @Get('admin')           // GET /auth/admin
  @UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard, RolesGuard)
  @Roles(Role.ADMIN)
  getAdminDashboard() { }
}

// Resource controller with project-scoped operations
@Controller('projects')
export class ProjectsController {
  constructor(private readonly projectsService: ProjectsService) {}

  @Post()
  @HttpCode(HttpStatus.CREATED)
  @UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)
  async create(@Body() dto: CreateProjectDto, @Request() req: { user: SafeUser }) {
    return this.projectsService.create(dto, req.user.id);
  }

  @Get()
  @UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)
  async findAll(@Request() req: { user: SafeUser }, @Query() query: PaginationQueryDto) {
    return this.projectsService.findByUser(req.user.id, query);
  }

  @Get(':id')
  @UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard, ProjectMemberGuard)
  async findOne(@Param('id') id: string) {
    return this.projectsService.findById(id);
  }

  @Delete(':id')
  @UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard, ProjectMemberGuard)
  @Roles(MemberRole.OWNER)
  async archive(@Param('id') id: string) {
    return this.projectsService.archive(id);
  }
}
```

### Request/Response Patterns

- **JSON Format**: Use JSON for request and response bodies
- **Consistent Structure**: Maintain consistent response structure across all endpoints
- **Status Codes**: Use appropriate HTTP status codes with `@HttpCode()` decorator

```typescript
// Success response (auth endpoints)
{
  "accessToken": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIs...",
  "refreshToken": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIs...",
  "user": {
    "id": "uuid",
    "email": "user@example.com",
    "role": "USER",
    "provider": "LOCAL"
  }
}

// Simple success
{
  "message": "Logged out successfully"
}
```

### Error Response Format

- **Consistent Format**: All errors follow the same response structure via `HttpExceptionFilter`
- **Error Codes**: Meaningful error codes mapped from HTTP status codes
- **HTTP Status Codes**: Map errors to appropriate HTTP status codes

```typescript
// Error response format (from HttpExceptionFilter)
{
  "success": false,
  "error": {
    "message": "Email already registered",
    "code": "CONFLICT",
    "statusCode": 409
  }
}

// Validation error (400)
{
  "success": false,
  "error": {
    "message": "Validation failed",
    "code": "VALIDATION_ERROR",
    "statusCode": 400,
    "details": [
      "Password must be at least 8 characters",
      "Password must contain at least one uppercase letter"
    ]
  }
}
```

**Error Code Mapping:**
| HTTP Status | Code | Usage |
|---|---|---|
| 400 | VALIDATION_ERROR | Input validation failures |
| 401 | UNAUTHORIZED | Missing or invalid auth token |
| 403 | FORBIDDEN | Insufficient permissions or account locked |
| 404 | NOT_FOUND | Resource not found |
| 409 | CONFLICT | Duplicate resource (e.g., email) |
| 500 | INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR | Unexpected server errors |

### CORS Configuration

- **Enable CORS**: Configure CORS via NestJS's `enableCors()` method in `main.ts`
- **Secure Configuration**: Only allow specific origins in production
- **Environment-Based**: Use environment variable for frontend URL

```typescript
// main.ts
app.enableCors({
  origin: process.env.FRONTEND_URL || 'http://localhost:3001',
});
```

## Database Patterns

### Prisma Schema

- **Single Source of Truth**: `prisma/schema.prisma` is the single source of truth for database structure
- **Consistent Naming**: Use PascalCase for models, camelCase for fields
- **Defaults**: Define sensible defaults for fields (e.g., `@default(uuid())`, `@default(now())`)
- **Enums**: Define enums in the schema and import them in TypeScript

```prisma
generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
}

enum Role {
  USER
  ADMIN
  SUPERADMIN
}

enum Provider {
  LOCAL
  GOOGLE
  GITHUB
}

model User {
  id             String    @id @default(uuid())
  email          String    @unique
  passwordHash   String?
  firstName      String?
  lastName       String?
  avatar         String?
  role           Role      @default(USER)
  provider       Provider  @default(LOCAL)
  providerId     String?
  emailVerified  Boolean   @default(false)
  failedAttempts Int       @default(0)
  lockedUntil    DateTime?
  isActive       Boolean   @default(true)
  createdAt      DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime  @updatedAt

  refreshTokens      RefreshToken[]
  ownedProjects      Project[]        @relation("ProjectOwner")
  projectMemberships ProjectMember[]
  // ... additional relations documented in data-model.md

  @@map("users")
}
```

> **Note:** The complete Prisma schema with all 15 models and 12 enums is documented in `data-model.md`. The schema above shows the User model as a representative example.

### Migrations

- **Descriptive Names**: Use descriptive migration names (`npx prisma migrate dev --name add_user_lockout`)
- **Review Before Applying**: Always review generated SQL before applying
- **Never Edit**: Never manually edit migration files after they've been applied
- **Seed Data**: Use `prisma/seed.ts` for development seed data

### PrismaService Pattern

PrismaService extends PrismaClient and integrates with NestJS lifecycle hooks for proper connection management.

```typescript
@Injectable()
export class PrismaService extends PrismaClient implements OnModuleInit, OnModuleDestroy {
  async onModuleInit(): Promise<void> {
    await this.$connect();
  }

  async onModuleDestroy(): Promise<void> {
    await this.$disconnect();
  }
}
```

**Module Registration:**
```typescript
@Global()
@Module({
  providers: [PrismaService],
  exports: [PrismaService],
})
export class PrismaModule {}
```

**Best Practice**: The `@Global()` decorator makes PrismaService available to all modules without explicit imports. Use `OnModuleInit` and `OnModuleDestroy` lifecycle hooks for connection management.

## Testing Standards

The project has strict requirements for code quality and maintainability. These are the unit testing standards and best practices that must be applied.

### Test File Structure
- Use descriptive test file names: `[componentName].spec.ts`
- Place test files in `tests/` directories within each module
- Use Jest as the testing framework with TypeScript support (ts-jest)
- Maintain coverage thresholds: branches 85%, functions 90%, lines 90%, statements 90%

### Test Organization Pattern
Template:
```typescript
describe('[ComponentName] - [methodName]', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    jest.clearAllMocks();
  });

  describe('should_[expected_behavior]_when_[condition]', () => {
    it('should [specific test case]', async () => {
      // Arrange
      // Act
      // Assert
    });
  });
});
```

Real example using NestJS TestingModule:
```typescript
describe('AuthService', () => {
  let authService: AuthService;
  let usersService: jest.Mocked<UsersService>;
  let jwtService: jest.Mocked<JwtService>;

  beforeEach(async () => {
    jest.clearAllMocks();

    const module: TestingModule = await Test.createTestingModule({
      providers: [
        AuthService,
        {
          provide: UsersService,
          useValue: {
            findByEmail: jest.fn(),
            findById: jest.fn(),
            create: jest.fn(),
            updateRefreshToken: jest.fn(),
          },
        },
        {
          provide: JwtService,
          useValue: {
            sign: jest.fn(),
            verify: jest.fn(),
          },
        },
      ],
    }).compile();

    authService = module.get<AuthService>(AuthService);
    usersService = module.get(UsersService);
    jwtService = module.get(JwtService);
  });

  describe('register', () => {
    it('should register a new user successfully', async () => {
      // Arrange
      const dto: RegisterDto = { email: 'test@example.com', password: 'Test1234!' };
      usersService.findByEmail.mockResolvedValue(null);
      usersService.create.mockResolvedValue(mockUser);
      jwtService.sign.mockReturnValue('mock-token');

      // Act
      const result = await authService.register(dto);

      // Assert
      expect(result.user.email).toBe(dto.email);
      expect(usersService.findByEmail).toHaveBeenCalledWith(dto.email);
    });

    it('should throw ConflictException when email exists', async () => {
      usersService.findByEmail.mockResolvedValue(mockUser);

      await expect(authService.register(mockDto)).rejects.toThrow(ConflictException);
    });
  });
});
```

**Service testing with Prisma transactions:**
```typescript
describe('ProjectsService - create', () => {
  it('should create project with owner membership and subscription', async () => {
    // Arrange
    const dto: CreateProjectDto = { name: 'My Project' };
    const userId = 'user-uuid';
    prisma.$transaction.mockImplementation((cb) => cb(prisma));
    prisma.project.create.mockResolvedValue(mockProject);
    prisma.projectMember.create.mockResolvedValue(mockMember);
    prisma.subscription.create.mockResolvedValue(mockSubscription);

    // Act
    const result = await service.create(dto, userId);

    // Assert
    expect(result.name).toBe(dto.name);
    expect(prisma.projectMember.create).toHaveBeenCalledWith(
      expect.objectContaining({ data: expect.objectContaining({ role: MemberRole.OWNER }) }),
    );
    expect(prisma.subscription.create).toHaveBeenCalledWith(
      expect.objectContaining({ data: expect.objectContaining({ plan: BillingPlan.FREE }) }),
    );
  });
});
```

### Test Case Naming Convention
- Use descriptive, behavior-driven naming: `should_[expected_behavior]_when_[condition]`
- Group related test cases under descriptive `describe` blocks
- Use snake_case for describe blocks and camelCase for individual tests

### Test Structure (AAA Pattern)
Always follow the Arrange-Act-Assert pattern:
```typescript
it('should return user profile when valid JWT provided', async () => {
  // Arrange - Set up test data and mocks
  const userId = 'test-uuid';
  const mockUser = { id: userId, email: 'test@example.com', role: Role.USER };
  usersService.findById.mockResolvedValue(mockUser);

  // Act - Execute the function under test
  const result = await authService.validateUser(userId);

  // Assert - Verify the expected behavior
  expect(result).toEqual(mockUser);
  expect(usersService.findById).toHaveBeenCalledWith(userId);
});
```

Assertion pattern:
- Use specific matchers: `toHaveBeenCalledWith()`, `toHaveBeenCalledTimes()`
- Verify both successful operations and error conditions
- Check that mocks were called with correct parameters
- Assert on return values and side effects

### Mocking Standards

- Mock all external dependencies using NestJS `TestingModule`
- Mock service layers in controller tests
- Mock PrismaService in service tests
- Use `jest.Mocked<T>` for type-safe mock declarations
- Clear all mocks in `beforeEach()` to ensure test isolation

```typescript
// Type-safe mock declaration
let usersService: jest.Mocked<UsersService>;

// Mock provider in TestingModule
{
  provide: UsersService,
  useValue: {
    findByEmail: jest.fn(),
    create: jest.fn(),
  },
}

// Get typed mock from module
usersService = module.get(UsersService);
```

### Test Coverage Requirements

- **Comprehensive test coverage**: Include these test categories for each function:
1. **Happy Path Tests**: Valid inputs producing expected outputs
2. **Error Handling Tests**: Invalid inputs, missing data, database errors
3. **Edge Cases**: Boundary values, null/undefined inputs, empty data
4. **Validation Tests**: Input validation, business rule enforcement
5. **Integration Points**: External service calls, database operations

- **Thresholds**: branches 85%, functions 90%, lines 90%, statements 90%
- **Coverage Reports**: Generate coverage reports with `npm run test:cov`
- **Excluded**: `main.ts`, `*.module.ts`, `prisma/` directory

### ESLint test-file relaxations (canonical pattern, established by SCRUM-359)

E2E and unit-test files in `nexacore-api` operate on Jest mocks, supertest responses, and dynamic fixtures where the strict `recommendedTypeChecked` ruleset (in particular the `@typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-*` family) generates noise without value. Production code in `src/` keeps the full ruleset; tests get a scoped relaxation in `eslint.config.mjs`:

```js
{
  // SCRUM-359: test directories are not production code. E2E tests intentionally
  // operate on loosely-typed Jest mocks and supertest responses, so the unsafe-*
  // family is noise here. Production code under src/ remains at full strictness.
  files: [
    '**/tests/**/*.ts',
    '**/*.spec.ts',
    'test/**/*.ts',
    'test/**/*.e2e-spec.ts',
  ],
  rules: {
    '@typescript-eslint/unbound-method': 'off',
    '@typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-argument': 'off',
    '@typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-assignment': 'off',
    '@typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-call': 'off',
    '@typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-member-access': 'off',
    '@typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-return': 'off',
    '@typescript-eslint/require-await': 'off',
    '@typescript-eslint/no-misused-promises': 'off',
    '@typescript-eslint/no-require-imports': 'off',  // Jest dynamic re-import pattern
    'security/detect-non-literal-require': 'off',
    'security/detect-non-literal-regexp': 'off',
    'security/detect-possible-timing-attacks': 'off',
  },
},
```

**Rules**:

- The override **must** include the `test/` glob (covers `test/auth-e2e/`) AND the `**/tests/**` glob (covers `src/**/tests/`). Missing either causes ~340 false-positive errors in `test/auth-e2e/` (root cause of the SCRUM-359 incident).
- New unit tests in production directories use `*.spec.ts` extension; new E2E tests use `*.e2e-spec.ts`. Both naming patterns are covered by the override.
- Do NOT relax these rules globally in `src/`. The Express boundary friction that often motivates relaxation has a canonical fix instead — see "Express type augmentation" below.

### Express type augmentation (canonical pattern, established by SCRUM-359)

NestJS guards, filters, and the throttler access `request.user`, `request.cookies`, `request.headers`, and a custom `request.oauthAction` field at the Express boundary. Without proper typing, every access generates `@typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-*` errors, tempting developers to either cast with `as any` (loses type-safety) or relax the rule globally (defeats the purpose). The canonical fix is **module augmentation** in `nexacore-api/src/types/express.d.ts`:

```ts
import type { OAuthAction } from '../auth/stores/oauth-state.store';

declare global {
  namespace Express {
    interface User {
      id: string;
      // ... other minimal properties needed for pre-auth code paths
    }
    interface Request {
      oauthAction?: OAuthAction;
      // ... other custom properties set by guards before auth completes
    }
  }
}

export {};
```

**Usage pattern** in guards/filters/throttlers:

```ts
import type { Request } from 'express';

async canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): Promise<boolean> {
  const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest<Request>(); // ← typed
  const userId = request.user?.id; // ← properly typed; no unsafe-* error
  // ...
}
```

For authenticated controllers, prefer the existing `AuthenticatedRequest` interface (`common/interfaces/authenticated-request.interface.ts`) which narrows `user` to the full `SafeUser` shape. The minimal `Express.User { id }` augmentation is intentionally permissive to allow pre-auth code (e.g., `OAuthLinkGuard`) to set `request.user = { id }` before the full user object is loaded.

This pattern is documented in `@types/passport`, used by Auth0 / Microsoft TS-Node-Starter / NestJS official samples, and was applied to 6 boundary files during SCRUM-359 (commit `4c47b53`) to eliminate 23 unsafe-* errors without rule relaxation.

