### SUPERADMIN Role Policy

The `SUPERADMIN` role is the **root role** of the EM Ecosystem. It represents the highest level of authority across the entire platform — both within EM NexaCore and across all satellite applications.

#### Access Scope

- **Full access to all Core modules**: Auth, Users, Projects, Teams, Notifications, Billing, Settings, Modules, Apps, Permissions — no restrictions.
- **Full access to all Satellite Apps**: SUPERADMIN has unrestricted access to every registered satellite application and its resources.

#### Automatic Guard Bypass

SUPERADMIN automatically bypasses authorization guards. All generated guards **must** include a SUPERADMIN check before evaluating other conditions:

- **`RolesGuard`**: Returns `true` immediately when the requesting user has `Role.SUPERADMIN`.
- **`PermissionsGuard`**: Returns `true` immediately when the requesting user has `Role.SUPERADMIN`.

```typescript
// Guard implementation pattern — SUPERADMIN bypass
@Injectable()
export class RolesGuard implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const user = request.user;

    // SUPERADMIN bypasses all role checks
    if (user?.role === Role.SUPERADMIN) {
      return true;
    }

    const requiredRoles = this.reflector.getAllAndOverride<Role[]>(ROLES_KEY, [
      context.getHandler(),
      context.getClass(),
    ]);
    if (!requiredRoles) return true;
    return requiredRoles.includes(user?.role);
  }
}
```

```typescript
// PermissionsGuard — same bypass pattern
@Injectable()
export class PermissionsGuard implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const user = request.user;

    // SUPERADMIN bypasses all permission checks
    if (user?.role === Role.SUPERADMIN) {
      return true;
    }

    const requiredPermissions = this.reflector.getAllAndOverride<string[]>(PERMISSIONS_KEY, [
      context.getHandler(),
      context.getClass(),
    ]);
    if (!requiredPermissions) return true;
    return requiredPermissions.every(perm => user?.permissions?.includes(perm));
  }
}
```

#### Management Capabilities

SUPERADMIN can manage all platform resources without explicit permission assignments:

| Resource              | Operations                                      |
|-----------------------|-------------------------------------------------|
| Users                 | Create, read, update, delete, assign roles      |
| Roles                 | Create, read, update, delete, assign to users   |
| Permissions           | Create, read, update, delete, assign to roles   |
| Internal Modules      | Register, enable, disable, configure             |
| Satellite Apps        | Register, approve, revoke, configure             |
| Global Settings       | Read and update system-level settings            |
| Global Billing        | Manage subscriptions, invoices, plans            |

#### Implicit Permission Rule

SUPERADMIN **always has all permissions**, even if they are not explicitly assigned in the `permissions` table. Services and guards must treat `Role.SUPERADMIN` as having every permission in the system — both existing and future permissions.

```typescript
// Service-level permission check pattern
function hasPermission(user: SafeUser, permission: string): boolean {
  if (user.role === Role.SUPERADMIN) return true;
  return user.permissions.includes(permission);
}
```

#### Agent and Code Generation Rules

All AI agents **must** follow these rules when generating code related to roles, guards, or permissions:

1. **Guard generation**: Every generated `RolesGuard` and `PermissionsGuard` must include the SUPERADMIN bypass as the first condition in `canActivate()`.
2. **Test generation**: Every guard and protected endpoint test suite must include specific SUPERADMIN test cases:
   - `'should allow SUPERADMIN to access regardless of required roles'`
   - `'should allow SUPERADMIN to access regardless of required permissions'`
3. **Endpoint documentation**: When documenting protected endpoints (OpenAPI, JSDoc, or markdown), always note that SUPERADMIN has unrestricted access.
4. **Module generation**: When generating new NexaCore modules, the generated guards must respect the SUPERADMIN bypass.
5. **Satellite App generation**: When generating satellite apps, the `NexaCoreAuthModule` must propagate the SUPERADMIN role from the JWT claims and enforce the same bypass logic.
6. **Permission generation**: When defining new permissions for a module or app, never require explicit assignment for SUPERADMIN — the bypass is implicit.
7. **OpenAPI generation**: Protected endpoints must include a note in their description: `SUPERADMIN: full access (bypasses all guards)`.

```typescript
// Required SUPERADMIN test cases for every guard
it('should allow SUPERADMIN to access regardless of required roles', () => {
  reflector.getAllAndOverride.mockReturnValue([Role.ADMIN]);
  const context = createMockContext({ role: Role.SUPERADMIN });
  expect(guard.canActivate(context as never)).toBe(true);
});

it('should allow SUPERADMIN to access regardless of required permissions', () => {
  reflector.getAllAndOverride.mockReturnValue(['projects:delete']);
  const context = createMockContext({ role: Role.SUPERADMIN, permissions: [] });
  expect(guard.canActivate(context as never)).toBe(true);
});
```

## Development Workflow

### Git Workflow

#### Branch Strategy

- **Base Branch**: `main` is the single source of truth. All feature branches are created from `main` and merged back into `main`.
- **Branch Naming**: `feature/[ticket-id]-backend` (e.g., `feature/SCRUM-12-backend`). The `-backend` suffix is mandatory to separate concerns when backend and frontend work happens in parallel for the same ticket.
- **Branch Scope**: One ticket = one branch. Keep branches small and focused on a single feature or fix. Do not mix unrelated changes.

#### Branch Lifecycle

```
main ─────────────────────────────────────────────── main
  │                                                    ▲
  └── feature/SCRUM-12-backend ── commits ── PR ── merge
```

1. **Create**: Branch from latest `main` → `git checkout -b feature/SCRUM-12-backend`
2. **Develop**: Implement changes following the ticket plan
3. **Commit**: Descriptive commit messages in English (see Commit Standards below)
4. **Push**: Push branch to remote → `git push -u origin feature/SCRUM-12-backend`
5. **PR**: Create Pull Request via `gh pr create` linking the ticket ID
6. **Review**: Minimum 1 approval required before merging
7. **Merge**: Squash merge into `main` (clean linear history)
8. **Cleanup**: Branch is auto-deleted after merge (configure in GitHub repo settings)

#### Commit Standards

- **Language**: English only (per `base-standards.mdc`)
- **Format**: Imperative mood, short subject line (max 72 chars), optional body with details
- **Ticket Reference**: Include ticket ID in subject or body (e.g., `SCRUM-12: Add billing module endpoints`)
- **Scope**: Stage only files related to the ticket. Never commit `.env`, secrets, or build artifacts
- **Tool**: Use `gh` CLI for all GitHub operations (PRs, reviews, checks)

#### Pull Request Standards

- **Title**: `[SCRUM-XX] Short description` (e.g., `[SCRUM-12] Add billing module CRUD endpoints`)
- **Description**: Summary of changes, link to Jira ticket, testing notes
- **Review**: Minimum 1 approval required. Reviewer checks: tests pass, lint clean, types valid, follows standards
- **Merge Strategy**: Squash merge — all commits in the branch are squashed into a single commit on `main`
- **Auto-delete**: Enable "Automatically delete head branches" in GitHub repo settings

#### Multi-Branch Coordination (Backend + Frontend)

When a ticket requires both backend and frontend changes:

1. **Backend first**: Create `feature/SCRUM-12-backend`, implement, PR, merge to `main`
2. **Frontend second**: Create `feature/SCRUM-12-frontend` from updated `main`, implement, PR, merge to `main`
3. **Reason**: Frontend depends on API endpoints, so backend must land first
4. **Exception**: If backend and frontend are fully independent, they can be developed in parallel

#### Conflict Resolution

- **Before PR**: Rebase your branch on latest `main` → `git pull --rebase origin main`
- **During PR**: If conflicts appear, pull latest `main`, resolve locally, push
- **Never force-push to `main`**. Force-push to feature branches only if necessary

#### Post-Merge Verification

- After merge, verify that `main` branch CI passes (lint, tests, build)
- If CI fails on `main`, fix immediately with a hotfix branch (`hotfix/SCRUM-XX-fix`)

